":"Lassina Zerbo","desc":"former Prime Minister of Burkina Faso, geophysicist, Executive Secretary of the Comprehensive Nuclear-Test-Ban Treaty Organization (CTBTO)","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/CTBTO_Executive_Secretary_Lassina_Zerbo.jpg/330px-CTBTO_Executive_Secretary_Lassina_Zerbo.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1963-10-10","died":null,"birthplace":"Bobo-Dioulasso","parties":[],"education":["University of Paris-Sud"],"occupations":["geophysicist","politician"],"wiki":"Lassina Zerbo"},"Q110666454":{"id":"Q110666454","name":"Paul-Henri Sandaogo Damiba","desc":"Burkinabé military officer and president (January–September 2022)","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Le_lieutenant-colonel_Paul_Henri_Sandaogo_Damiba%2C_Ouagadougou_le_27_janvier_2022_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Le_lieutenant-colonel_Paul_Henri_Sandaogo_Damiba%2C_Ouagadougou_le_27_janvier_2022_%28cropped%29.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1981-01-02","died":null,"birthplace":"Ouagadougou","parties":[],"education":["École Militaire"],"occupations":["military personnel","politician","military officer"],"wiki":"Paul-Henri Sandaogo Damiba"},"Q111131079":{"id":"Q111131079","name":"Albert Ouédraogo","desc":"Burkinabe politician","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Albert_Ou%C3%A9draogo_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Albert_Ou%C3%A9draogo_%28cropped%29.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1969-04-06","died":null,"birthplace":"Dori","parties":[],"education":["Prytanée militaire de Kadiogo","University Joseph Ki-Zerbo"],"occupations":["politician","minister"],"wiki":"Albert Ouédraogo"},"Q114341246":{"id":"Q114341246","name":"Ibrahim Traoré","desc":"military leader of Burkina Faso since 2022","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Capitaine_Ibrahim_Traor%C3%A9.jpg/330px-Capitaine_Ibrahim_Traor%C3%A9.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1988-03-14","died":null,"birthplace":"Bondokuy","parties":[],"education":["University Joseph Ki-Zerbo"],"occupations":["military officer","politician"],"wiki":"Ibrahim Traoré"},"Q114835874":{"id":"Q114835874","name":"Apollinaire Joachim Kyélem de Tambèla","desc":"Prime Minister of Burkina Faso","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Apollinaire_Kyelem_de_Tamb%C3%A8la_%28cropped%29.png/330px-Apollinaire_Kyelem_de_Tamb%C3%A8la_%28cropped%29.png","gender":"M","born":"1958-06-11","died":null,"birthplace":"Koupéla","parties":[],"education":[],"occupations":["politician","lawyer","writer","television presenter"],"wiki":"Apollinaire J. Kyélem de Tambèla"},"Q116056730":{"id":"Q116056730","name":"Jean Emmanuel Ouédraogo","desc":"Burkinabe politician","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Jean-Emmanuel_Ou%C3%A9draogo.jpg/330px-Jean-Emmanuel_Ou%C3%A9draogo.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1980-12-26","died":null,"birthplace":"Tenkodogo","parties":[],"education":["University Joseph Ki-Zerbo"],"occupations":["politician","journalist"],"wiki":"Jean Emmanuel Ouédraogo"}},"summary":"Burkina Faso is a landlocked country in West Africa. It is bordered by Mali to the northwest, Niger to the northeast, Benin to the southeast, Togo and Ghana to the south, and Ivory Coast to the southwest. It covers an area of 274,223 km2 (105,878 sq mi). In 2024, the country had an estimated population of approximately 23,286,000. Its citizens are known as Burkinabes, and its capital and largest city is Ouagadougou.\nThe largest ethnic group in Burkina Faso is the Mossi people, who settled the area in the 11th and 13th centuries. They established kingdoms such as Ouagadougou, Tenkodogo, and Yatenga. In 1896, it was colonised by the French as part of French West Africa; in 1958, it became a self-governing colony within the French Community. In 1960, it gained full independence with the name of the Republic of Upper Volta, with Maurice Yaméogo as the president. Since it gained its independence, the country has dealt with political instability, droughts, famines, and corruption. There have been various coups d'état, in 1966, 1980, 1982, 1983, 1987, and twice in 2022, in January and September. There were also unsuccessful coup attempts in 1989, 2015, and 2023. \nBurkina Faso remains one of the least developed countries in the world, with a GDP of $16.226 billion in 2022. Approximately 63.8% of its population practices Islam, while 26.3% practices Christianity and 9% animistic religions, especially in the southwest of the country. The country's official languages include Mooré, Dyula, Fula, and Bissa (as of 1998), with Mooré being spoken by over half the population. There are more than 60 indigenous languages, and the constitution provides for other languages to be made official by law. The former government and business language was French until January 2024 when, alongside English, its status was demoted to that of a \"working language\" by ratification of a constitutional amendment.\nThe country's territory is geographically biodiverse, and includes plentiful reserves of gold, manganese, copper and limestone. Due to its multicultural make-up, Burkinabe art has a rich and long history, and is renowned for its orthodox style. \nThe country is governed as a semi-presidential republic, with executive, legislative and judicial powers. It is a member of the United Nations and the Organisation of Islamic Cooperation. In 2025, Burkina Faso withdrew from ECOWAS and La Francophonie, after it helped form the Alliance of Sahel States (AES). It also remains suspended from the African Union.","history":"The history of Burkina Faso includes the history of various kingdoms within the country, such as the Mossi kingdoms, as well as the later French colonisation of the territory and its independence as the Republic of Upper Volta in 1960.","historyTitle":"History of Burkina Faso","curated":null,"indicators":{"gdpPerCapita":[[1960,69.1502],[1961,72.2424],[1962,77.1529],[1963,78.9088],[1964,80.9148],[1965,82.0746],[1966,82.8361],[1967,84.6272],[19